        "The reported outcome was that the panel identifies what new evidence could decide. It would be tempting to read this as the narrative’s moral: an obstacle recognised, an adjustment made, a difficulty overcome. Yet such a reading confuses resolution with closure. A change may be defensible without being optimal, and a persuasive account may remain epistemically incomplete. The more demanding conclusion is therefore conditional and reversible: the response appears reasonable on the evidence provided, while the untold alternatives, the excluded voices and the possibility of unintended consequences continue to qualify that judgement.",
